{#
  NAVBAR PARTIAL
  --------------
  An "include" (see base.njk, which does {% include "partials/navbar.njk" %})
  is a smaller, reusable chunk of template that gets pasted into a
  bigger one at render time — same idea as a {% block %}, but it's the
  layout choosing to pull in a piece, rather than a page filling in a
  slot the layout defined.

  Every value here comes from `locale.site`, which is the per-language
  data built in site.ar.json / site.en.json (loaded by home.js). That
  file holds things that are the same shape on every page — nav links,
  logos, footer text — so as more pages get migrated in later phases,
  they can all reuse this exact partial instead of re-writing a navbar
  by hand in every template like the old site did.

  Every link/image path below is prefixed with locale.pathPrefix (see
  layouts/base.njk's header comment) so this same partial still points
  at the right places from a nested page like a Year Archive
  (/years/2025.html), not just from root-level pages.
#}
<!-- NAVBAR -->
{#
  data-pagefind-ignore (Phase 3): base.njk's <body data-pagefind-body>
  makes the whole page indexable by default; this excludes just the nav
  itself so every page's search result isn't padded with the same 16
  nav-link labels. See base.njk's header comment for the full picture.
#}
<nav class="navbar navbar-expand-lg navbar-light sticky-top" data-pagefind-ignore>
<div class="container d-flex align-items-center">

  {#
    aria-label here is conditional on locale.site.brand.ariaLabel being
    non-empty — kept that way for flexibility, even though both
    languages currently do set a real value. Worth knowing the history:
    early on (Home, Contract, Schedule), NEITHER language's Arabic page
    had this aria-label, only English did. Migrating the Submissions
    page surfaced that ITS original Arabic navbar already had one — so
    rather than reproduce the gap on every other page too, a real
    Arabic aria-label was adopted here for all pages going forward
    (see the "_comment" note at the top of site.ar.json).
  #}
  {#
    Both logos, side by side (2026-08-18 navbar revision). Previously
    the association logo lived all the way at the other end of the
    bar, in the assoc-fixed cluster with the utility icons — paired
    with the brand mark here instead, since they're both "who this
    is" (organizer + partner), not actions. Frees the right-hand
    cluster to be pure actions/utilities (Register, language, search,
    theme, menu). The association logo keeps its own external link to
    taqa.org.sa; it's just adjacent to the brand link now, not merged
    into one link.
  #}
  <div class="navbar-brands d-flex align-items-center">
    <a class="navbar-brand d-flex align-items-center" href="{{ locale.pathPrefix | default('') }}{{ locale.site.brand.href }}"{% if locale.site.brand.ariaLabel %} aria-label="{{ locale.site.brand.ariaLabel }}"{% endif %}>
      <img src="{{ locale.pathPrefix | default('') }}assets/logos/taqathon-logo.png" alt="{{ locale.site.brand.logoAlt }}" class="logo-mark">
    </a>
    <span class="navbar-divider" aria-hidden="true"></span>
    <a href="{{ locale.site.assoc.href }}" target="_blank" rel="noopener"{% if locale.site.assoc.linkClass %} class="{{ locale.site.assoc.linkClass }}"{% endif %}{% if locale.site.assoc.ariaLabel %} aria-label="{{ locale.site.assoc.ariaLabel }}"{% endif %}>
      <img src="{{ locale.pathPrefix | default('') }}assets/logos/association-logo.png" class="assoc-logo" alt="{{ locale.site.assoc.alt }}">
    </a>
  </div>

  <div class="collapse navbar-collapse" id="mainNav">
    <ul class="navbar-nav mb-2 mb-lg-0 align-items-lg-center">
      {#
        aria-current="page" tells screen readers which nav item matches
        the page currently open — a real accessibility improvement, not
        just cosmetic. It was only ever present on the original
        Schedule (English) page's active link, missing everywhere else
        on the site by accident. Since this loop is now shared by every
        migrated page, adding it here once fixes that inconsistency
        retroactively for Home and Contract too, not just Schedule.

        This loop is the entire reason a navbar partial is worth having:
        one {% for %} over a list, instead of many near-identical
        <li><a>...</a></li> lines typed out by hand (and kept in sync by
        hand across every page, in both languages, forever). Adding a
        new nav link later means adding one entry to site.*.json — this
        loop just picks it up automatically.

        The "active" class marks whichever link matches the current
        page. `locale.navActive` is set per-page in each page's data
        file (e.g. home.js sets it to "home"); it's compared against
        each nav item's own `key` from site.*.json.

        Phase 3 (2026-08-11): the flat list of links grew to 16 items
        and needed grouping, so each entry now has a "type" —
          - "link": exactly what this loop always rendered.
          - "dropdown": a Bootstrap navbar dropdown (toggle + submenu)
            wrapping several related "items"; the toggle itself isn't
            a real link (no page behind "About" or "Community" as a
            GROUP — About's own page is one of the items inside it),
            so it's rendered as a <button>-role link with
            data-bs-toggle="dropdown", not an <a href>.
          - "button": a single highlighted call-to-action (Register).
            Rendered OUTSIDE this loop now (see the navbar-actions
            block below) — user feedback (2026-08-18) was that having
            it inline as just another <li> made it disappear into the
            packed row of nav links instead of reading as a distinct
            CTA. Still declared here in site.*.json (so its href/label
            stay data-driven, not hardcoded), just skipped by this
            loop and picked out separately afterward.
        A dropdown's toggle gets the "active" treatment too, so
        landing on e.g. Tracks (inside the "About" group) still shows
        SOMETHING highlighted in the top-level bar, not nothing.
      #}
      {% for item in locale.site.nav %}
        {% if item.type == "dropdown" %}
          {% set groupActive = false %}
          {% for sub in item.items %}
            {% if sub.key == locale.navActive %}{% set groupActive = true %}{% endif %}
          {% endfor %}
          <li class="nav-item dropdown">
            <a class="nav-link dropdown-toggle{% if groupActive %} active{% endif %}" href="#" id="navDropdown-{{ item.key }}" role="button" data-bs-toggle="dropdown" aria-expanded="false">
              {{ item.label | safe }}
            </a>
            <ul class="dropdown-menu" aria-labelledby="navDropdown-{{ item.key }}">
              {% for sub in item.items %}
              <li><a class="dropdown-item{% if sub.key == locale.navActive %} active{% endif %}" href="{{ locale.pathPrefix | default('') }}{{ sub.href }}"{% if sub.key == locale.navActive %} aria-current="page"{% endif %}>{{ sub.label | safe }}</a></li>
              {% endfor %}
            </ul>
          </li>
        {% elif item.type == "link" %}
          <li class="nav-item"><a class="nav-link{% if item.key == locale.navActive %} active{% endif %}" href="{{ locale.pathPrefix | default('') }}{{ item.href }}"{% if item.key == locale.navActive %} aria-current="page"{% endif %}>{{ item.label | safe }}</a></li>
        {% endif %}
      {% endfor %}
    </ul>

    {#
      Mobile drawer utilities (2026-08-18 navbar revision, round 3).
      User feedback: the mobile top bar (2 logos + Register + language
      switch + search + theme + hamburger, all always visible) made
      the whole site read as cramped/small — too many small targets
      competing for a 375px-wide row. Fix: below the lg breakpoint,
      the persistent top bar now shows only the logos, a compact
      Register button, and the hamburger (see navbar-mobile-actions
      below) — language switch, search, and theme toggle move IN HERE,
      inside the collapsible drawer alongside the nav links, reachable
      with one tap on the hamburger instead of permanently competing
      for space in the top bar. d-lg-none hides this block entirely at
      the lg breakpoint, where the ORIGINAL always-visible copies of
      these three controls (in .assoc-fixed below) take over instead —
      see base.njk's theme-toggle script for how having two copies of
      that particular button is kept in sync.
    #}
    <div class="navbar-drawer-utils d-flex d-lg-none align-items-center gap-2">
      <a class="btn btn-sm btn-lang" href="{{ locale.altHref }}"{% if locale.site.langSwitch.ariaLabel %} aria-label="{{ locale.site.langSwitch.ariaLabel }}"{% endif %}>{{ locale.site.langSwitch.label }}</a>
      <button type="button" class="btn btn-sm btn-search-trigger" data-bs-toggle="modal" data-bs-target="#searchModal" aria-label="{{ locale.site.search.triggerAriaLabel }}">
        <i class="bi bi-search"></i>
      </button>
      <button type="button" class="btn btn-sm btn-theme-toggle" aria-pressed="false" aria-label="{{ locale.site.darkModeToggleAriaLabel }}">
        <i class="bi bi-moon-stars"></i>
      </button>
    </div>
  </div>

  {#
    Desktop action cluster (Register CTA + language switch, divider,
    utility icons) — lg and up only (d-none d-lg-flex). Below lg, this
    entire cluster is replaced by navbar-mobile-actions further down
    and the drawer copies above; see the navbar-drawer-utils comment
    for the full reasoning.

    Nav-links centering (2026-08-18, round 3): .navbar-collapse gets
    flex:1 and justify-content:center at the lg breakpoint (see
    common.css) so the link group sits in the middle of the space
    between the logos and this cluster, rather than hugging the logos
    on the left — the standard "logo — centered nav — CTA" layout most
    marketing/event sites use, and a layout that stays visually
    balanced at any viewport width instead of depending on a fixed
    margin value.
  #}
  <div class="assoc-fixed d-none d-lg-flex align-items-center">
    {% for item in locale.site.nav %}
      {% if item.type == "button" %}
      <a class="btn nav-register-btn{% if item.key == locale.navActive %} active{% endif %}" href="{{ locale.pathPrefix | default('') }}{{ item.href }}"{% if item.key == locale.navActive %} aria-current="page"{% endif %}>
        <i class="bi bi-pencil-square"></i> {{ item.label | safe }}
      </a>
      {% endif %}
    {% endfor %}
    <a class="btn btn-sm btn-lang" href="{{ locale.altHref }}"{% if locale.site.langSwitch.ariaLabel %} aria-label="{{ locale.site.langSwitch.ariaLabel }}"{% endif %}>{{ locale.site.langSwitch.label }}</a>
    <span class="navbar-divider" aria-hidden="true"></span>
    <button type="button" class="btn btn-sm btn-search-trigger" data-bs-toggle="modal" data-bs-target="#searchModal" aria-label="{{ locale.site.search.triggerAriaLabel }}">
      <i class="bi bi-search"></i>
    </button>
    <button type="button" class="btn btn-sm btn-theme-toggle" aria-pressed="false" aria-label="{{ locale.site.darkModeToggleAriaLabel }}">
      <i class="bi bi-moon-stars"></i>
    </button>
  </div>

  {#
    Mobile action cluster: below lg, this is the ONLY thing that stays
    permanently visible next to the logos — a compact Register button
    (still the primary CTA, so it stays reachable without opening the
    menu) and the hamburger itself. Everything else lives one tap away
    in the drawer above. margin-inline-start:auto pushes this flush to
    the far end on its own, since .assoc-fixed (which normally carries
    that margin) is display:none at this width and can't push anything.
  #}
  <div class="navbar-mobile-actions d-flex d-lg-none align-items-center gap-2">
    {% for item in locale.site.nav %}
      {% if item.type == "button" %}
      <a class="btn nav-register-btn{% if item.key == locale.navActive %} active{% endif %}" href="{{ locale.pathPrefix | default('') }}{{ item.href }}"{% if item.key == locale.navActive %} aria-current="page"{% endif %}>
        <i class="bi bi-pencil-square"></i> {{ item.label | safe }}
      </a>
      {% endif %}
    {% endfor %}
    {#
      aria-controls / aria-expanded aren't language-specific, so they're
      always included now; only the aria-label TEXT comes from data
      (locale.site.toggler.ariaLabel). Same history as the brand link
      above — early pages' Arabic navbar had none of this at all.
    #}
    <button class="navbar-toggler" type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse" data-bs-target="#mainNav" aria-controls="mainNav" aria-expanded="false" aria-label="{{ locale.site.toggler.ariaLabel }}">
      <span class="navbar-toggler-icon"></span>
    </button>
  </div>

</div>
</nav>
